ry evidence becomes available","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"2a27eab2-64fa-5c87-aa73-f584e054f0d2","text":"Organisms have no evolutionary history","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"2ab98802-2c2f-51c4-8737-25ac166082bc","text":"All taxonomists must use five kingdoms","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"3f057d12-011b-59e1-a850-b794352ad1a6","position":56,"prompt":"What is binomial nomenclature?","explanation":"The two-part scientific name supports consistent identification across languages and regions.","questionKind":"concept","difficulty":"easy","cognitive":"recall","topic":"Classification and Diversity of Living Things","fingerprint":"cf3594c32360cecf25e5f14d05084fceafb99b871cbfa00288aacd4234f830d1","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"General Biology I enrichment: Classification and Diversity of Living Things","sourceKind":"general_biology1","reviewedFromLocalFile":false,"databaseCourseCode":"BIO 101","sourceCourseCode":"BIO 111"},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_bio101_from_bio111_300_v1","sourceReference":"General Biology I enrichment: Classification and Diversity of Living Things","technicalAccuracyReviewCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"d193a984-5c86-5106-aa58-ad030b594d56","text":"Naming a species with a genus name and a specific epithet","isCorrect":true,"position":0},{"id":"07a8a3fc-15d9-5075-b893-d79b4dcb6505","text":"Giving every organism one common nickname","isCorrect":false,"position":1},{"id":"685f7e84-8f61-508a-b721-b328673fe537","text":"Classifying only by colour","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"be10e3a8-d742-52fe-9e6b-2bc2460f005b","text":"Naming an organism after its habitat alone","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"a24e3956-fa00-58d0-956a-48315c1727fc","position":57,"prompt":"Which three domains are widely recognized in modern classification?","explanation":"The three-domain framework reflects major evolutionary divisions revealed by molecular evidence.","questionKind":"application","difficu