ect on either species","isCorrect":true,"position":2},{"id":"d0ca9f15-bc06-5652-b6e7-6c06d13dfc55","text":"Both species gain an obligatory benefit","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"f653cd83-9682-58f4-a53a-59c6d369a0e9","position":287,"prompt":"What is amensalism?","explanation":"Trampling grass can harm the plant while having negligible effect on the animal.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Biological Interactions and Community Ecology","fingerprint":"bbffca2d9fc0684b3aa73a9cf8b6a81355d1fbc6335247d1488b124b3674711e","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"BIO 111 General Biology I module, Study Session 15","sourceKind":"supplied_bio111_material","reviewedFromLocalFile":true,"databaseCourseCode":"BIO 101","sourceCourseCode":"BIO 111"},"generationMeta":{"import":"codex_bio101_from_bio111_300_v1","sourceReference":"BIO 111 General Biology I module, Study Session 15","technicalAccuracyReviewCompleted":true,"humanVerificationRequired":true},"options":[{"id":"c50885c6-c147-5cb1-966d-4fd965cbe1e8","text":"Both are unaffected","isCorrect":false,"position":0},{"id":"2df05a66-f91b-52b6-9aa4-b9373f0f49b7","text":"One species is harmed while the other is unaffected","isCorrect":true,"position":1},{"id":"dbf72dc5-1b25-54e1-8650-9700a68f7a45","text":"Both species benefit","isCorrect":false,"position":2},{"id":"d438930a-0593-585c-9f1e-97976bd4dc18","text":"One benefits and one is harmed","isCorrect":false,"position":3}]},{"id":"edb2a9f3-6d5b-5ca5-bc1a-12a83c0417a7","position":288,"prompt":"What is competition?","explanation":"Competitors reduce each other's access to food, space, mates or other scarce resources.","questionKind":"application","difficulty":"medium","cognitive":"application","topic":"Biological Interactions and Community Ecology","fingerprint":"964d273acd679c2d5f90afc4f87c44e7ba71d7a430257a45d5a0ea2f93b1c117","studyRef":{"sourceLabel":"BIO 111 General Biology I module, Study Session 15","sourceKind":"supplied_bio111_ma